# UX Audit Playbook

Structure: **Modules × Checks matrix.** Walk each module in scope, apply each check, record findings.

---

## When to run

- Before a major release (feature-complete gate)
- When UX drift is suspected (design system out of sync)
- Onboarding a new designer — capture current state
- Cadence every N months for established products

---

## Preparation

1. List modules in scope. A module ≈ a screen, flow, or domain area.
2. Open the design system / style guide.
3. Have the product running locally (or staging).
4. Screen-record the walkthrough if possible — useful for re-tests.

---

## Checks matrix

For each module × each check, note whether it passes, fails, or is not applicable. Failures become findings in `00-inventory.md`.

### Core checks (apply to every module)

| # | Check | Fail = finding type |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Visual consistency** — typography, spacing, colors match the design system | `bug` or `gap` |
| 2 | **Interactive states** — hover, focus, active, disabled, loading all defined | `bug` |
| 3 | **Empty states** — what does the user see when there's no data? | `gap` |
| 4 | **Error states** — every failure path shows a message the user understands | `gap` or `bug` |
| 5 | **Responsive** — works at mobile (375), tablet (768), desktop (1280), wide (1920) | `bug` |
| 6 | **Keyboard navigation** — all actions reachable, focus visible, shortcuts documented | `bug` |
| 7 | **Copy** — clear, consistent voice, no placeholder lorem, no "TODO" strings | `bug` or `idea` |
| 8 | **Loading feedback** — any operation >200ms has a loading indicator | `bug` or `gap` |
| 9 | **Destructive action guardrails** — confirmations, undo where possible | `risk` |
| 10 | **First-run / onboarding** — new users understand what to do | `gap` |
| 11 | **Text truncation** — clipped text either wraps or exposes its full value on hover/focus; never an ellipsis with neither | `bug` |

**Check 11 in practice.** The recurring case is **table column headers**: a header truncated
to `Fecha de creaci…` with no `title`, no tooltip and no wrap leaves the column unnameable,
and it is the kind of defect a person spots in one second and an audit that does not name
it walks straight past. Same defect, same verdict on: truncated cell values, nav items,
buttons and tabs, breadcrumbs, and any label inside a fixed-width chip or badge. Resize the
viewport and lengthen the content — a header that fits the seed data is not evidence.

A tooltip is a pass only if it is reachable by keyboard focus, not hover alone; otherwise
the finding stands and belongs to the a11y run as well.

### Advanced checks (apply selectively)

| # | Check | When |
|---|---|---|
| 12 | **Dark mode parity** | If dark mode is offered |
| 13 | **Internationalization** — strings externalized, RTL tested | If i18n is shipped |
| 14 | **Print stylesheet** | If users print |
| 15 | **Browser parity** — Chrome, Safari, Firefox, mobile Safari | Pre-release |
| 16 | **Animation / motion** — respects `prefers-reduced-motion` | If heavy animation |

---

## Post-implementation style-consistency comparison

Run this **after** a page or component ships, on the new surface only. It is not a repeat
of core check 1: that one compares against the design system as written, which a new page
can satisfy in the abstract while still looking visibly unlike its own neighbours. This one
compares against **a concrete sibling that already shipped** — the established page a user
would see immediately before or after the new one.

1. **Name the reference in `index.md`.** One existing page or component, chosen because a
   user actually moves between it and the new surface (the list a form is reached from; the
   card that already exists in the same grid). Unnamed, "compare against an established
   reference" is unfalsifiable and the step quietly becomes a no-op.
2. **Put them side by side at the same viewport** — same zoom, same theme, same data density.
   Screenshot both; the pair is the evidence.
3. **Walk the drift axes**, in this order — earlier ones are the ones people notice:
   - alignment and grid — do the two share a left edge, gutter and column rhythm?
   - vertical rhythm — heading sizes, section spacing, density of the same kind of row
   - control shape — button height, radius, icon size, input height
   - color roles — is the accent used for the same job in both?
   - state and interaction — hover, focus and disabled read the same way
   - copy register — sentence case vs title case, terminology for the same object
4. **Repeat at one narrow viewport.** Drift that hides at desktop width is common.

Drift becomes findings, one row each, using the format under
[Recording findings](#recording-findings):

- **misalignment or a mismatched control against the reference** → `bug`
- **an established pattern the reference has and the new surface omits** (empty state,
  loading skeleton, row action affordance) → `gap`

Two guardrails. If the reference itself is the outlier, say so and pick another — this
comparison propagates whatever it is pointed at. And drift that turns out to be a
deliberate divergence is not a finding: record the decision instead, and name it in
`index.md` so the next run does not re-raise it.

---

## Recording findings

Each failure becomes a row in `00-inventory.md`:

```
| BUG-<MODULE>-<N> | bug | <module> | <one-line summary> | open | P<N> | {{DATE}}-<slug> | — | — |
```

Severity guide:
- **P0** — blocks usage (broken flow, illegible text)
- **P1** — degrades trust (inconsistent UI, wrong errors, mobile broken)
- **P2** — annoying but usable (minor visual drift, awkward copy)
- **P3** — polish (micro-animations, rare edge cases)

---

## Output artifacts

- `index.md` — this run's context, scope, auditor, summary
- `findings.md` — filtered view linking to `00-inventory.md`
- Optional: `modules/<name>.md` — per-module notes if the audit is large

---

## Tips

- **Timebox.** One hour per module works for most products. If you need more, split into two runs.
- **Two-pass.** First pass collect findings fast. Second pass triage severity.
- **Photo evidence.** Attach screenshots in `modules/<name>/` when the issue is visual.
- **Don't fix on the fly.** This audit catalogs; fixes go to backlog or plans via `/aidex-audit escalate`.
